1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,696
The instructor will not give the next step. Do not wait for a hint.
State your current concern, identify missing information and choose
the next safe action that fits your role and the applicable pathway.

2
00:00:12,696 --> 00:00:25,800
A partner should be able to tell what you are doing and why. Use
names, closed-loop requests and a brief confirmation. When new
evidence arrives, revise openly rather than defending the first plan.

3
00:00:25,800 --> 00:00:42,312
An original two-patient drill begins with one person who is breathless
and tiring, and another who looks relatively calm but has become pale
with a weaker peripheral pulse. The call contains no dramatic alarm,
and the full histories are not yet available.

4
00:00:42,312 --> 00:00:59,160
Do not rank patients by who speaks loudest. Compare immediate airway,
breathing and perfusion threats, evaluate whether one can be safely
supported while help arrives, and make a resource request early.
Reassess both patients after each major action.

5
00:00:59,160 --> 00:01:15,720
Halfway through the drill, the primary oxygen source stops delivering.
Announce the equipment failure immediately and keep the patient under
direct observation. The team should maintain appropriate airway or
ventilation support while the approved backup is prepared.

6
00:01:15,720 --> 00:01:34,368
The person switching equipment follows the actual service checklist
and training. The lead verifies the replacement source and reassesses
the patient rather than assuming that a successful connection equals
clinical improvement. If the patient continues to tire, escalate care
and transport planning.

7
00:01:34,368 --> 00:01:50,184
During treatment, one patient says they feel better and now refuses
transport. A family member protests. Pause tasks that can safely wait,
address the patient directly and assess the decision in the context of
the current condition.

8
00:01:50,184 --> 00:02:09,000
A symptom change does not prove the risk is gone. Explain the
remaining uncertainty and options, ask the patient to describe their
understanding and personal reasoning, and consult according to current
guidance. Respect a capable decision while keeping care available and
documenting the process.

9
00:02:09,000 --> 00:02:23,160
Before the next action, give a short team update: name the leading
threat, state who is doing what and specify the finding that will
trigger a change. This creates a shared mental model and makes missed
steps easier to catch.

10
00:02:23,160 --> 00:02:36,000
Avoid vague directions such as “keep an eye on them.” Name the
observation, its timing and the person responsible. If the patient
changes, re-state the priority and close the communication loop.

11
00:02:36,000 --> 00:02:52,584
The learner begins the debrief with three questions: What happened
from your point of view? Which cue changed your plan, and how did you
use it? What would you repeat or change next time? Ask the learner to
cite observable evidence, not just a feeling of success or failure.

12
00:02:52,584 --> 00:03:08,688
The facilitator then adds questions about patient response, team
coordination, equipment readiness, consent and documentation. Separate
a knowledge gap from an execution gap: one may need review, the other
deliberate supervised practice.

13
00:03:08,688 --> 00:03:23,784
Use the remaining debrief prompts to examine the patient perspective,
cue quality, alternative explanations, priority choice, authorized
actions and patient response. Ask which assumption was useful and
which one needed testing.

14
00:03:23,784 --> 00:03:39,312
End with one specific behaviour to practise, a condition under which
it will be tested and a way to know it improved. “Be more confident”
is not measurable. “State the risk and one alternative before
documenting refusal” is observable.

15
00:03:39,312 --> 00:04:03,936
After the learner has asked the first three debrief questions,
separate the action from the patient result. A verified source
replacement is a process achievement. What happened to breathing,
interaction and perfusion is an outcome question. Both matter. An
incomplete observation must remain unknown, not be filled with an
assumed improvement because the device now works.

16
00:04:03,936 --> 00:04:28,344
A useful review links cue, interpretation, chosen action, expected
response and actual response. Include the patient's account and what
mattered to them. If several interventions preceded an improvement,
the sequence alone does not prove that one action caused every change.
Describe the evidence before deciding what the team should repeat or
change next time.

17
00:04:28,344 --> 00:04:54,792
Reconstruct the two-patient drill without adding findings. The
breathless person is tiring, while the quieter person has worsening
perfusion cues. Calm appearance cannot settle priority. Form
provisional explanations, compare immediate threats and available
support, and identify what would change the plan. New evidence may
require revising an earlier priority rather than defending the first
choice.

18
00:04:54,792 --> 00:05:21,048
The six judgment functions help show what happened: notice and analyze
cues, compare hypotheses, generate feasible options, take an
authorized action and evaluate. They overlap during care. A normal
finding may still be relevant, and a plausible explanation is not
confirmed simply because a treatment was given. Do not wait to
complete every label before addressing a threat that cannot wait.

19
00:05:21,048 --> 00:05:50,856
Environmental constraints include equipment, staffing, access to
services, setting and time pressure. Clinician factors include
cognitive load, fatigue, experience and actual skill. A plan that
ignores these can fail even when its physiology sounds reasonable.
Name the specific gap, request appropriate help and confirm who
accepted responsibility. An unfamiliar task is not solved by quietly
improvising beyond training.

20
00:05:50,856 --> 00:06:18,240
Culture and communication should prompt respectful questions about
this person, not a diagnosis by group membership. Check the actual
history, relevant records and preferences and support understanding.
In the debrief, ask whether an assumption hid a useful cue or a needed
resource. Patient involvement and accessible explanation are part of
reasoning, not optional decorations after the technical work.

21
00:06:18,240 --> 00:06:46,608
The chapter's unfolding analysis adds pain, function, skin and
elimination concerns after an initial respiratory problem. Learn to
reopen the assessment rather than copy the hospital orders. Lack of
wound pain does not prove healing, and a new symptom is not
automatically arthritis, embarrassment or the bed. Keep chronology and
associated findings visible and use the appropriate clinical pathway
for the new concern.

22
00:06:46,608 --> 00:07:10,440
In our drill, do not forget the second patient while troubleshooting
the first person's equipment. Assign continuing observation and a
clear escalation trigger for both. A symptom that improves does not
erase other risks or settle a refusal decision. Handover should
include the unresolved need and who will reassess it, not only the
tasks that were completed.

23
00:07:10,440 --> 00:07:36,576
ADPIE organizes assessment, diagnosis, planning, implementation and
evaluation. The judgment model makes selected cognitive work more
visible; it was not built to replace the nursing process or become an
AET marking rubric. Use both ideas to explain a decision without
claiming that naming a model proves competence. A useful goal
specifies what will be observed and when it will be reviewed.

24
00:07:36,576 --> 00:08:01,056
Choose one change for the reset, such as clearly assigning and
reporting a patient reassessment after source replacement. Keep the
other variables stable enough to see what changed. Review the observed
behavior and the actual response without blaming hindsight or
inventing success. Carry forward the next specific practice target and
leave formal skill assessment with the school's supervised process.

25
00:08:01,056 --> 00:08:18,912
After the debrief, repeat a short segment with only one variable
changed: the equipment fails sooner, the second patient worsens or the
patient gives a less clear explanation. Avoid changing every detail at
once; the learner needs to see which adjustment mattered.

26
00:08:18,912 --> 00:08:31,584
This weekend revisits existing knowledge and skills. It does not add a
new technical skill station. Record the learner’s next practice target
and carry it into the next school session as directed.
